## Summary

The q-scaled hidden-terminal construction works cleanly as a finite-horizon minimax lower-bound atom. It proves that if an unknown class contains two laws that are observationally identical for \(D\) quote rounds and differ only in a terminal valuable-tail event of probability \(q\), then every learner suffers regret \(\Omega(qD)\) on one law.

This supports the lower-bound mechanism “valuable delayed reward plus indistinguishability,” but it does not by itself give matching power-tail rates. For a power envelope \(\beta(h)\lesssim h^{-\alpha}\), the one-block construction only yields \(\Omega(D^{1-\alpha})\) at horizon \(T=D+1\), far below the known truncation upper rate for many \(\alpha\).

## Concrete Progress

Define horizon \(T=D+1\). In both worlds,
\[
P_1=\cdots=P_D=0.
\]
At \(T\), world \(L\) draws
\[
P_T=\begin{cases}
0,&1-q,\\
Y_L\sim {\rm Unif}[1/2,5/8],&q,
\end{cases}
\]
and world \(H\) draws
\[
P_T=\begin{cases}
0,&1-q,\\
Y_H\sim {\rm Unif}[3/4,7/8],&q.
\end{cases}
\]

For a fixed quote \(a\), define base terminal revenue curves
\[
f_i(a)=a\Pr(Y_i>a).
\]
Then
\[
\sup_a f_L(a)=1/2,\qquad \sup_a f_H(a)=3/4,
\]
while
\[
\sup_a \frac{f_L(a)+f_H(a)}2=1/2.
\]
The q-scaled curves are just \(q f_i(a)\), so the Bayes gap is
\[
q\left(\frac{1/2+3/4}{2}-\frac12\right)=q/8
\]
per early quote.

Thus every learner has regret at least
\[
\frac{qD}{8}
\]
on one of the two worlds.

The tail quantities are also explicit. For either world,
\[
\beta(h)=
\begin{cases}
q,& h<D,\\
0,& h\ge D,
\end{cases}
\]
up to integer/strict-inequality conventions.

For integer truncation \(H<D\),
\[
\Delta_L(H)=\delta_L(H)=q(D-H)\cdot \frac12,
\]
\[
\Delta_H(H)=\delta_H(H)=q(D-H)\cdot \frac34.
\]
So the lower bound is of the same \(qD\) scale as the long-tail truncation mass when \(H\) is a fixed fraction below \(D\).

## Claims Or Lemmas

**Lemma 1: General hidden-terminal Bayes-gap lower bound.**

Let two laws be identical through rounds \(1,\dots,D\), with all early prices equal to zero, and differ only through a terminal distribution \(\mu_0,\mu_1\) at round \(D+1\). Let
\[
g_i(a)=a\mu_i((a,1]),
\qquad
V_i=\sup_a g_i(a),
\]
and
\[
\Gamma=\frac{V_0+V_1}{2}-\sup_a \frac{g_0(a)+g_1(a)}2.
\]
Then every randomized learner has regret at least \(D\Gamma\) in one of the two worlds.

**Lemma 2: q-scaling is linear.**

If \(\mu_i=(1-q)\delta_0+q\nu_i\), then \(g_i=q f_i\), \(V_i=q\sup_a f_i(a)\), and \(\Gamma=q\Gamma_0\). For the interval pair above, \(\Gamma_0=1/8\), hence lower bound \(qD/8\).

**Lemma 3: Tail and truncation profile of q-scaled terminal pair.**

For the q-scaled terminal pair, max delay is \(D\), raw tail mass is \(q\) for all \(h<D\), and the singleton-law truncation losses satisfy
\[
\delta_i(H)=\Delta_i(H)=q(D-H)_+V_i^0.
\]

## Proof Attempts

The lower bound is direct. Before terminal time, both worlds generate the same observed history, so the learner’s distribution over \(a_t\) is world-independent for every \(t\le D\). Under the uniform prior over worlds, expected reward from any early quote is at most
\[
q\sup_a \frac{f_L(a)+f_H(a)}2=q/2.
\]
The average comparator value per early quote is
\[
q\frac{\sup_a f_L(a)+\sup_a f_H(a)}2=q\cdot\frac58.
\]
Thus average regret is at least \(q/8\) per early quote, hence \(qD/8\). Therefore one world has regret at least \(qD/8\).

For rate matching, the same construction gives: if a nonincreasing envelope \(B(h)\) is imposed and \(q\le B(D)\), then the class satisfies \(\beta(h)\le B(h)\) and gives lower bound \(\Omega(D B(D))\). For \(B(h)=h^{-\alpha}\), this is only \(\Omega(D^{1-\alpha})\).

## Gaps And Risks

The q-scaled atom is finite-horizon and class-level. It is not a fixed-law asymptotic necessity theorem.

It does not match the existing upper rate for power tails. The construction forces \(q\le \beta(D)\) because keeping \(D\) decisions unresolved requires delay \(D\). Hence it naturally gives \(D\beta(D)\), not the optimized upper tradeoff.

Repeating blocks is not automatically valid. If block types are iid, the fixed comparator may collapse to the Bayes action. If all blocks share one type, the first successful reveal can teach the learner the type, limiting accumulated regret.

A stronger lower bound likely needs either multi-scale hidden information that is not fully revealed by one terminal draw, or a Le Cam/Fano-style construction where each reveal carries limited information while the optimal quote remains separated.

## Counterexamples Or Obstructions

Naive iid block-splicing remains obstructed: the regret comparator is a single fixed quote in expectation, so independent block types can erase the realized per-block optimal-action gap.

Known-law singleton versions are harmless: if the learner knows whether it is in \(L\) or \(H\), it simply plays the relevant revenue maximizer and has zero regret. The lower bound requires unknown/minimax class quantifiers.

For power tails, the one-block q-scaled construction is too weak for \(\alpha\ge 1\), giving at most constant or vanishing lower growth when \(q=D^{-\alpha}\).

## Sources Consulted

No web sources consulted.

